WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.400
Tool execution controls are where MCP security becomes operational.

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:07.066
Read-only tools and write-capable tools carry different risk.

00:00:07.066 --> 00:00:11.230
A read-only inventory query may expose sensitive data if poorly controlled,

00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:14.960
but a write-capable tool can modify files, send messages, create tickets,

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:18.780
change cloud resources, update repositories, or trigger workflows.

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:22.885
High-impact actions should never rely on the model alone to decide what is safe.

00:00:22.885 --> 00:00:27.330
Command execution, file modification, email, messaging, code repository changes,

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:31.040
cloud actions, ticketing, and workflow automation all need external enforcement.

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:33.300
The model can produce arguments or recommend an action,

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:35.900
but validation should happen in deterministic code.

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:38.500
Schema validation checks shape and type.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:40.889
Argument validation checks allowed values and constraints.

00:00:40.889 --> 00:00:43.270
Allowlisted operations limit what the tool can do.

00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:48.085
Policy checks decide whether this user, in this context, can request this operation.

00:00:48.085 --> 00:00:51.880
Transaction previews and dry-run modes help users understand consequences before

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:52.480
execution.

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:55.530
A preview can show what file will change, which ticket will be updated,

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:58.680
who will receive a message, what cloud resource will be modified,

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:00.800
or which repository branch is affected.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:04.995
Human approval gates are especially important for irreversible, costly, external,

00:01:04.995 --> 00:01:06.670
or privileged actions.

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:08.975
Approval should be tied to the specific action and arguments,

00:01:08.975 --> 00:01:11.190
not just to a generic permission granted earlier.

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:13.010
Rollback planning completes the control story.

00:01:13.010 --> 00:01:17.190
If a tool can make changes, the team should know how to undo or contain mistakes.

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:20.875
That may involve version history, reversible workflows, backups, change records,

00:01:20.875 --> 00:01:23.310
rate limits, or incident response procedures.

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:27.505
Safe operations assume that models can misunderstand context and users can approve too

00:01:27.505 --> 00:01:28.035
quickly.

00:01:28.035 --> 00:01:31.760
The system should make the safe path easy and enforce hard limits outside the model.
